@prefix pidsys: <http://data.ten.com/ont/pidsys#> .
@prefix ido:    <http://rds.posccaesar.org/ontology/lis14/rdl/> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:    <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:   <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#################################################################
#  Workstream 2 — the pidsys: structural / pre-commissioning
#  extension ontology (net-new, bounded per
#  ido_semantic_mapping_spec.md §6.1)
#
#  Naming decision (2026-09-15): the spec drafts this vocabulary
#  as "precomm:"; the real running code (gold/vocab.py,
#  rdf_mapper.py, and data already pushed to live Fuseki) uses
#  "pidsys:" for the identical set of terms. This file matches the
#  code — an ontology describes what is emitted, so pidsys: is
#  authoritative and ido_semantic_mapping_spec.md §6.1/§6.3 should
#  be reconciled to pidsys:, not the reverse.
#
#  IDO-alignment discipline (Gold spec §4.3, risk #2): a term is
#  anchored to IDO ONLY when verified present; otherwise it is
#  project-owned under pidsys:, never fabricated.
#
#  IMPORT LAYER — PINNED (2026-09-16) to IDO/LIS-14 v4.2 (FDIS):
#    prefix  ido: <http://rds.posccaesar.org/ontology/lis14/rdl/>
#    imports      <http://rds.posccaesar.org/ontology/lis14/ont/core>
#  Source file: LIS-14-for-FDIS.ttl (owl:versionIRI .../core/4.2,
#  "4.2 FDIS proposal submitted April 2026", dcterms:modified
#  2026-04-10). This SUPERSEDES the earlier plan to pin to the 2021
#  RDL core: v4.2 is the current PCA artifact, same ontology IRI and
#  same lis: term namespace, and every ido: term this file uses was
#  audited against it directly (below). The 2023 WD (WD_IDO.pdf) is
#  now only historical context; where 4.2 and the WD differ, 4.2 wins.
#
#  KEY 4.2 FACTS (audited from the file, not inferred):
#    - PhysicalArtefact ⊑ PhysicalObject  — NOT ⊑ InanimatePhysicalObject.
#      4.2 changed this deliberately ("to allow for artificially made
#      organisms"). Our PipingComponent/Equipment ⊑ PhysicalArtefact
#      still resolves to PhysicalObject, just not via InanimatePhysical.
#    - Feature ⊑ PhysicalObject  — CONFIRMED (explicit). Nozzle/Node
#      connectivity is legal.
#    - System ⊑ FunctionalObject, and System is disjoint from NOTHING.
#      The only PhysicalObject disjointness is the set
#      {InformationObject, Location, Organization, PhysicalObject}.
#      So functional-only PipingSegment/Line is permitted (not
#      unsatisfiable), and Connection/OffPageConnector ⊑ InformationObject
#      IS enforced against being physical.
#    - FunctionalObject is NOT deprecated in 4.2 (full definition,
#      ⊑ Object + hasFunction some Function). The deprecation we tracked
#      earlier was 2023-WD-only; hasMember's no-explicit-range design is
#      still correct, just no longer for deprecation reasons.
#    - connectedTo: Symmetric, Domain/Range PhysicalObject;
#      directlyConnectedTo ⊑ connectedTo, Symmetric.
#    - hasFunctionalPart: ⊑ hasPart, Domain System, Range FunctionalObject.
#    - hasFeature: ⊑ hasArrangedPart, Range Feature.
#    - installedAs: does NOT exist in 4.2 — we use it in no axiom (prose
#      only, already corrected), so no impact. The physical→functional
#      link is hasFunction/realizedIn (both present in 4.2).
#  Anchors used (audited against 4.2):
#    - ido:PhysicalArtefact — the made physical items:
#      pidsys:PipingComponent and pidsys:Equipment (4.2:
#      PhysicalArtefact ⊑ PhysicalObject directly; a made physical
#      object — e.g. a bolt, a pressure vessel). More specific than
#      bare PhysicalObject and resolves to it directly.
#    - ido:Feature — pidsys:ConnectionNode, a point/part on a
#      component (WD: "a contiguous, non-separable part of a physical
#      object"; examples "a flange face, ... a bolt threading").
#      ido:Feature is a subclass of ido:PhysicalObject (confirmed
#      2026-09-15), so a node is legally on the connectedTo graph.
#    - ido:System — the grouping/output classes (ProcessUnit,
#      StartUpPackage, InstrumentationLoop, CommissioningSystem) and
#      PipingSegment/Line subclass it DIRECTLY, no intermediate root
#      (WD: lis:System SubClassOf hasFunctionalPart some
#      FunctionalObject; example "a pressure vessel, a crane, a
#      pipeline").
#    - ido:InformationObject — Connection and OffPageConnector (WD:
#      "a pattern that is about something, concretized as at least
#      one physical entity"; example "a P&ID diagram").
#  Membership: pidsys:hasMember ⊑ ido:hasFunctionalPart (WD: Domain
#  System, Range FunctionalObject). The physical→functional link is
#  the FUNCTION-realization pattern (a functional individual
#  hasFunction some Function; that Function, being a Potential, is
#  realizedIn some Activity in which the physical object participates)
#  — NOT ido:installedAs. NOTE (4.2): ido:installedAs does not exist in
#  v4.2 at all; we use it in no axiom (an earlier draft wrongly cited
#  it as the physical/functional bridge — corrected; the bridge is
#  hasFunction/realizedIn, both present in 4.2).
#  FUNCTIONALOBJECT STATUS (4.2): NOT deprecated — full definition,
#  ⊑ Object + hasFunction some Function. (An earlier note tracked a
#  `deprecated? true` flag; that was 2023-WD-only and does not hold in
#  the pinned 4.2.) pidsys:hasMember still declares NO explicit range
#  and inherits Range FunctionalObject from hasFunctionalPart — a
#  clean-inheritance choice, so the property follows the core if the
#  range ever changes; no longer a deprecation workaround.
#
#  CODE-DIVERGENCE FLAG: Gold spec §4.3 reports the running vocab.py
#  asserts ONLY ido:PhysicalObject and routes systems through a
#  project term. This file additionally uses ido:System,
#  ido:InformationObject, ido:connectedTo/directlyConnectedTo, and
#  the ido:hasFunctionalPart / hasFunction / realizedIn membership
#  pattern (no explicit FunctionalObject pin — see DEPRECATION above). Until vocab.py/rdf_mapper.py are updated, the
#  ontology is ahead of the emitted triples — a real reconciliation
#  item, logged in the scoping note, not to be silently ignored.
#################################################################

<http://data.ten.com/ont/pidsys>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "T.EN pre-commissioning systemization extension (pidsys)" ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.2.0-draft (2026-09-16; pinned to IDO/LIS-14 v4.2 FDIS)" ;
    rdfs:comment """Structural and relational vocabulary for the Automatic
Pre-commissioning Systemization data product: a reified Connection, flow
direction, the off-page connector, connection-node identity, the boundary
role, and the grouping/output System classes. Deliberately small (per
ido_semantic_mapping_spec.md §6.1); the hard content is the rule layer,
which lives in SHACL/SPARQL/Jena over graph:refdata, not here. Anchors to
the IDO terms ido:PhysicalObject, ido:System, and ido:InformationObject
(term facts AUDITED against IDO/LIS-14 v4.2 FDIS, the pinned import — see
the file header for the per-term results; satisfiability still owed in a
reasoner run).
ido:InformationObject is the parent of BOTH the reified Connection and the
OffPageConnector (the latter a drawing symbol and a Connection ENDPOINT,
Connection's sibling, not its subclass and not a PhysicalObject) — this the
ontology ENFORCES, via the WD's PhysicalObject|InformationObject
disjointness. PipingSegment and Line are ido:System ONLY — functional
groupings (piperun-family) of components by shared characteristics
(diameter, piping class, insulation). This is a MODELLING choice on 4D
identity-basis grounds, NOT enforced: the WD does NOT declare System
disjoint from PhysicalObject, so a dual-typed segment would be permitted-
but-wrong. The physical pipe pieces are separate InanimatePhysicalObject
individuals. The systemization walk traverses physical adjacency at the
COMPONENT/PIECE level, so segments correctly stay off the physical
connectivity graph. Connectivity reuses the native symmetric
ido:connectedTo / ido:directlyConnectedTo (between physical pieces);
membership (pidsys:hasMember ⊑ ido:hasFunctionalPart) reaches physical
components through the FUNCTION-realization pattern (hasFunction /
realizedIn), NOT ido:installedAs (which is the tag/serial relation). Only
genuinely non-native terms are minted under pidsys: — the reified
Connection and OffPageConnector classes, PipingSegment/Line, flowsTo (no
directional IDO companion exists), node identity, the grouping/output
classes, and the boundary role.""" ;
    owl:imports <http://rds.posccaesar.org/ontology/lis14/ont/core> .

#################################################################
#  Anchor points (project-owned roots)
#################################################################

pidsys:PipingComponent
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:PhysicalArtefact ;
    rdfs:label "Piping Component" ;
    rdfs:comment """Root for every placed, genuinely PHYSICAL plant item
mapped by map_component — a single made artifact you could weld or ship (a
gate valve, a reducer, a flange). Anchored under ido:PhysicalArtefact
(4.2: PhysicalArtefact ⊑ PhysicalObject directly — 4.2 deliberately does
NOT place it under InanimatePhysicalObject, "to allow for artificially
made organisms") rather than bare ido:PhysicalObject — a piping component
is manufactured (made by humans), so PhysicalArtefact is the more
specific, more accurate anchor, and it resolves to PhysicalObject
directly. Real domain classes (GateValve, CheckValve,
PipeReducer, ...) are asserted rdfs:subClassOf this at projection time; a
class not yet resolved to a real RDL/PLM URI additionally carries
pidsys:rdlUriPending true rather than a fabricated URI. NOTE:
PipingSegment and Line are NOT rooted here — they are functional groupings
(ido:System), not single artefacts.""" .

pidsys:Equipment
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:PhysicalArtefact ;
    rdfs:label "Equipment" ;
    rdfs:comment """Separate physical hierarchy from PipingComponent, per
Gold spec §4.4: map_equipment asserts a project-minted equipment class
rdfs:subClassOf this, keeping equipment classification distinct from
piping-component classification. Anchored under ido:PhysicalArtefact — a
vessel, pump, or exchanger is a manufactured physical object; the WD's own
PhysicalArtefact example list includes "a pressure vessel".""" .

pidsys:Nozzle
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Nozzle" ;
    rdfs:comment """A connection point on a Process Equipment (spec §2.9.1)
— the flanged opening where a Piping Segment attaches to a vessel or
column. Anchored under ido:Feature (WD: "a contiguous, non-separable part
of a physical object"; examples "a flange face, the inside surface of a
pump"), the same anchor as ConnectionNode: a nozzle is a point ON
equipment, belonging to exactly one equipment item, not a made object in
its own right, so it is NOT a PhysicalArtefact. Related to its parent by
the native ido:featureOf (inverse of ido:hasFeature): Nozzle featureOf
Equipment. Because ido:Feature is a subclass of ido:PhysicalObject
(confirmed), a Nozzle is legally on the ido:connectedTo graph — which is
essential: "nozzles are how equipment enters the connectivity graph"
(§2.9.1), piping attaching node-to-node at the nozzle. Carries a
mandatory nozzle tag and nominal diameter (§2.9.1); always tagged (a
missing tag is a data-quality error). NB (§2.9): an Equipment element is
kept only if tagged AND bearing at least one nozzle — nozzle-less shells
are ghost equipment, flagged not silently dropped.

NOTE: the functional 'tag' aspect of a nozzle (the designated role 'N1')
is deliberately NOT modelled as a separate FunctionalObject — a
FunctionalObject is not a PhysicalObject and so would leave the
ido:connectedTo graph, which is exactly where a nozzle must sit. (This
holds independent of FunctionalObject's status; note it is NOT deprecated
in v4.2.) If the functional role is ever needed, attach ido:hasFunction
some ido:Function to this Feature rather than re-typing the nozzle.""" .

pidsys:PipingSegment
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:System ;
    rdfs:label "Piping Segment" ;
    rdfs:comment """A Piping Segment is a FUNCTIONAL grouping of piping
components that share characteristics (nominal diameter, piping materials
class, insulation purpose/thickness) — a piperun-family concept, which is
strictly functional. Under ISO 15926's 4D approach a functional object has
functional (not material) continuity as its basis for identity: the
functional requirement (the 'slot') is not the material (the 'filler'), so
the segment is the slot and the physical pipe pieces that realise it are
separate InanimatePhysicalObject individuals.

It is therefore modelled as ido:System ONLY — NOT dual-typed with
ido:InanimatePhysicalObject and NOT physical. IMPORTANT precision (audited
against IDO/LIS-14 v4.2 FDIS): this is a MODELLING choice on 4D
identity-basis grounds, NOT an axiom the ontology enforces. v4.2's
complete disjointness set is: {Dependent|Object|Temporal};
{InformationObject|Location|Organization|PhysicalObject};
{Activity|SpatiotemporalRegion|TemporalRegion}; plus pairwise
Organism|(implied) and a few others — but System is disjoint from
NOTHING, and PhysicalObject is NOT declared disjoint from System or
FunctionalObject. So a dual-typed segment would be permitted-but-wrong,
not unsatisfiable — the reasoner would accept it; the modeller must
decline it. (Contrast pidsys:OffPageConnector/Connection under
InformationObject, which the ontology DOES enforce against being physical,
via the {InformationObject|...|PhysicalObject} disjoint set.)

Connectivity consequence: the systemization walk (walk.py) traverses
physical adjacency at the COMPONENT/PIECE level, not segment-to-segment, so
the segment does not need to sit on the physical ido:connectedTo graph —
and correctly does not, since connectivity holds between physical
realizations. The segment groups those pieces via ido:hasFunctionalPart /
ido:hasAssembledPart and carries the shared diameter/class/insulation
attributes. map_line/map_segment (Gold spec §4.4) build this grouping over
the piece-level physical nodes the walk actually uses.""" .

pidsys:Line
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:System ;
    rdfs:label "Line (Pipeline System)" ;
    rdfs:comment """The functional grouping one level above PipingSegment
(Gold spec §4.4's bi-temporal versioning unit): PipingSegment
--functionalPartOf--> Line. This IS the master-data "Pipeline System"
(§2.2) — the two are the same concept, so no separate pidsys:PipelineSystem
class is minted; "Pipeline System" is the master-data name for this class.
Same strict reading as PipingSegment — ido:System only, a functional
grouping of segments by shared line-level characteristics, not a physical
object. map_line aggregates each of
fluid/unit/diameter/piping_materials_class/insulation as a tuple, since
member pieces can disagree.

Containment chain (spec): Document → Line (Pipeline System) → Subline
(Sub Piping System, project B) → PipingSegment → PipingComponent. Each
grouping level is ido:System; membership down the chain uses
ido:functionalPartOf / ido:hasFunctionalPart.""" .

pidsys:Subline
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:System ;
    rdfs:label "Sub Piping System (Subline)" ;
    rdfs:comment """The project-scoped grouping level between Line and
PipingSegment (spec §2.2a), present in project B (ISO-15926/bppidsys), not
project A. A functional grouping like the other line-hierarchy levels —
ido:System only. Sits in the containment chain: Line --hasFunctionalPart-->
Subline --hasFunctionalPart--> PipingSegment. Because it is project-scoped,
the projection asserts it only where the source data carries the subline
level; project A skips it and Line contains PipingSegment directly.""" .

pidsys:UnclassifiedComponent
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pidsys:PipingComponent ;
    rdfs:label "Unclassified Component" ;
    rdfs:comment """C_UNCLASSIFIED_COMPONENT — an honest 'no real
classification available' node for a component with no component_class or
one carrying a source-tool catch-all placeholder
(CATCHALL_COMPONENT_CLASSES). Never a fabricated guess.""" .

#################################################################
#  Documents (spec §2.0 / master-data object 1)
#################################################################

pidsys:Document
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:InformationObject ;
    rdfs:label "Document" ;
    rdfs:comment """A P&ID (and, more generally, an engineering document) —
master-data object 1 (spec). Anchored under ido:InformationObject (WD: "a
pattern that is about something, concretized as at least one physical
entity"; the WD's own example is "a P&ID diagram"). Declared explicitly
(rather than left as bare native InformationObject) so that Connection and
OffPageConnector — which are ABOUT a drawing — have a concrete class to
point at, and so the containment chain (Document → Line → ...) has a real
root node.

Document TYPE is deliberately NOT modelled as subclasses here. The kind of
a document (P&ID, isometric, GA, datasheet, cause-&-effect, ...) is
reference data, not T-Box: a project can generate a very large, evolving
set of document types, so they belong in a governed Document-Type Reference
Data Library (SKOS concepts in graph:refdata), with each Document carrying
a hasDocumentType link to a concept — the same rules-as-data discipline the
boundary roles and fluid catalogue follow. Minting an owl:Class per
document type would bake a mutable, project-specific list into the ontology.
See the scoping note for the RDL design.""" .

pidsys:hasDocumentType
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:Document ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "has document type" ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a Document to its type concept (P&ID, isometric,
datasheet, ...) held as governed SKOS reference data in graph:refdata — NOT
an owl:Class per type. Kept as an object property to a skos:Concept
precisely so the document-type vocabulary stays data, not T-Box: a project
generates a large, evolving, client-varying set of types, so minting a
class each would bake a mutable list into the ontology. Same rules-as-data
discipline as pidsys:hasBoundaryRole. Each concept may carry a crosswalk
URI to an external standard (CFIHOS / PCA RDL document types) and the same
resolution discipline as component classes (exact | close | label |
pending), so an unresolved type is honest rather than fabricated.""" .

#################################################################
#  Connectivity — the keystone reified edge (spec §3.1 gap 2,
#  §3.2 row 2.12)
#################################################################

pidsys:Connection
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:InformationObject ;
    rdfs:label "Connection" ;
    rdfs:comment """Keystone extension class. IDO's ido:connectedTo is a
bare, symmetric, unreified property and cannot carry the Source/Derived
provenance flag or a connection type — so the edge is reified. A reified
connection is an information artifact ABOUT two physical objects, not a
physical thing itself, so it is anchored under the native
ido:InformationObject (the same category the P&ID Document maps to) rather
than left under owl:Thing. IDO has no native reified-connection class
(spec §3.1), so the class is net-new, but its parent is native.
One instance per silver_connections row (map_connection), carrying
fromObject/toObject, fromNode/toNode, connType, and a MANDATORY derived
boolean. Being an InformationObject, a Connection is NOT itself connectable
via ido:connectedTo — correct: an edge is about things, not connected to
them.""" .

pidsys:ConnectionNode
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:Feature ;
    rdfs:label "Connection Node" ;
    rdfs:comment """A connection point on a component (spec §2.4.1) —
anchored under ido:Feature, the WD-native term for "a contiguous,
non-separable part of a physical object" (WD examples: "a flange face, the
inside surface of a pump, ... a bolt threading"). This is more accurate
than the prior ido:PhysicalObject anchor: a node is a point/feature ON a
component, not a made object in its own right, so it is NOT a
PhysicalArtefact and is a Feature, related to its component by
ido:hasFeature.

Node identity is net-new (it carries per-node nominal diameter and is what
a Connection's fromNode/toNode point at). The projection emits native
ido:connectedTo / ido:directlyConnectedTo for graph walking; the reified
pidsys:Connection alongside them carries the provenance (derived) and type.
No pidsys: connectivity property is minted.

CONNECTIVITY (confirmed): ido:connectedTo / ido:directlyConnectedTo are
domained on ido:PhysicalObject, and ido:Feature is a subclass of
ido:PhysicalObject (confirmed by David, 2026-09-15 — the WD's DL text does
not spell out Feature's SubClassOf, but Feature does sit under
PhysicalObject). So a ConnectionNode, as a Feature, is legally on the
connectivity graph and node-to-node directlyConnectedTo is valid. Node-
level adjacency is therefore sound; component/piece-level adjacency (what
walk.py uses) remains the primary path.""" .

pidsys:connectionType
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:Connection ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "connection type" ;
    rdfs:comment """One of the four types of the single Connection entity
(spec §2.12): Process, Nozzle attachment, Signal, Off-Page continuation.""" .

pidsys:derived
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:Connection ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "derived" ;
    rdfs:comment """Provenance flag (spec §2.12, [MD §2.12]). false = a
connection stated whole in the source; true = reconstructed (inferred
node-to-node links, matched off-page pairs). Mapping a Connection with no
derived value is a hard error in map_connection.""" .

pidsys:fromObject
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:Connection ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ;
                 owl:unionOf ( ido:PhysicalObject pidsys:OffPageConnector ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "from object" ;
    rdfs:comment """Endpoint of a reified Connection. Range is the union of
ido:PhysicalObject (piping component, nozzle, equipment) and
pidsys:OffPageConnector, because §2.12 admits an Off-Page Connector as a
valid endpoint — and an OPC is an InformationObject, not a PhysicalObject,
so the range cannot be PhysicalObject alone. Deliberately a union of the
two real endpoint types rather than widening to ido:Object/owl:Thing.""" .

pidsys:toObject
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:Connection ;
    rdfs:range [ a owl:Class ;
                 owl:unionOf ( ido:PhysicalObject pidsys:OffPageConnector ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "to object" ;
    rdfs:comment "Endpoint of a reified Connection; same union range as pidsys:fromObject." .

pidsys:fromNode
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:Connection ;
    rdfs:range pidsys:ConnectionNode ;
    rdfs:label "from node" .

pidsys:toNode
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:Connection ;
    rdfs:range pidsys:ConnectionNode ;
    rdfs:label "to node" .

pidsys:flowsTo
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain ido:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:range ido:PhysicalObject ;
    rdfs:label "flows to" ;
    rdfs:comment """Directed flow — the property IDO/LIS-14 lacks entirely
(spec §3.1 gap 1). Deliberately NOT a subproperty of ido:connectedTo:
connectedTo is symmetric, and a subproperty inherits symmetry, which would
destroy direction. IDO's core has no asymmetric/directional connectivity
companion (confirmed: directlyConnectedTo is connectedTo's only
subproperty, also symmetric), so flowsTo is a legitimate standalone
pidsys: term. Emitted ONLY when flow_sense orients the edge
(forward/reverse/both); flow_sense=none emits none at all, never a guessed
direction. The three directional guards (flare, consumer, relief
inlet-side) read this; without it they are not expressible.""" .

#################################################################
#  Off-Page Connector (spec §2.11; design in
#  off_page_connector_representation.md)
#################################################################

pidsys:OffPageConnector
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:InformationObject ;
    rdfs:label "Off-Page Connector" ;
    rdfs:comment """A drawing-continuation symbol (spec §2.11) — an
information artifact marking that a piping network continues elsewhere, NOT
a physical thing in the plant. Anchored under ido:InformationObject, a
SIBLING of pidsys:Connection, not a subclass of it and not a PhysicalObject:
- Not PhysicalObject — it is a symbol on a drawing, not plant equipment
  (deliberately excluded from equipment.rdf / ten_equipment.rdf).
- Not a subclass of Connection — an OPC is the ENDPOINT a Connection points
  at (§2.12 lists 'Off-Page Connector' as a valid from/to endpoint type,
  beside Piping Component and Nozzle), not the edge. Making it a Connection
  would invert the from/to model (edges pointing at edges). The mating-pair
  relationship IS a Connection (connType 'Off-Page continuation',
  derived=true) whose fromObject/toObject are the two OPC endpoint nodes;
  the connector node itself is this endpoint class.
Its DEXPI sandbox URI (e.g.
http://sandbox.dexpi.org/rdl/FlowOutPipeOffPageConnector) is passed through
as component_class_uri for traceability, not minted as a new class. Flow
direction (FlowIn/FlowOut...) is metadata on the node, not a subclass.""" .

pidsys:terminates
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:OffPageConnector ;
    rdfs:range pidsys:PipingSegment ;
    rdfs:label "terminates" ;
    rdfs:comment """The OPC terminates a Piping Segment (1:1, spec §2.13) —
range is pidsys:PipingSegment specifically, not the general PipingComponent
(a segment is a grouping, now its own dual-nature class). A DIRECT edge,
deliberately NOT reified through Connection — unlike the mating-pair
relationship, the spec gives this row no '(via Connection)' tag.""" .

pidsys:offPageFlowDirection
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:OffPageConnector ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "off-page flow direction" ;
    rdfs:comment """FlowInPipeOffPageConnector / FlowOutPipeOffPageConnector
— which end of the cross-document pair this node is. A qualifier, not a
type.""" .

#################################################################
#  Grouping / output System classes (spec §3.2, §3.3)
#################################################################

pidsys:ProcessUnit
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:System ;
    rdfs:label "Process Unit" ;
    rdfs:comment "Tag-decoded grouping level (spec §2.1a)." .

pidsys:StartUpPackage
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:System ;
    rdfs:label "Start-Up Package" ;
    rdfs:comment "Reference-data-provided grouping root (spec §2.1b)." .

pidsys:InstrumentationLoop
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:System ;
    rdfs:label "Instrumentation Loop" ;
    rdfs:comment "Instrument-function grouping (spec §2.8)." .

pidsys:CommissioningSystem
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf ido:System ;
    rdfs:label "Commissioning System" ;
    rdfs:comment """The computed systemization output (spec [SS]). All
members have 'computed' provenance and land in graph:results, produced by
the rule package AFTER the Silver walk — never asserted in the shared
plant model (graph:masterdata). The class is declared here; population is
the rule layer's job, not this ontology's.""" .

pidsys:hasStartUpPackage
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:ProcessUnit ;
    rdfs:range pidsys:StartUpPackage ;
    rdfs:label "has start-up package" .

pidsys:hasMember
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf ido:hasFunctionalPart ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:CommissioningSystem ;
    rdfs:label "has member" ;
    rdfs:comment """A computed commissioning system's members, as a
subproperty of the native ido:hasFunctionalPart — so membership
participates in IDO's own composition semantics (per the WD:
lis:hasFunctionalPart Domain lis:System; lis:System SubClassOf
lis:hasFunctionalPart some ...; lis:functionalPartOf is the inverse
direction).

RANGE BY INHERITANCE (v4.2): this property declares NO explicit rdfs:range;
it inherits Range lis:FunctionalObject from ido:hasFunctionalPart (v4.2:
hasFunctionalPart Domain System, Range FunctionalObject). In v4.2
lis:FunctionalObject is NOT deprecated (full definition, ⊑ Object +
hasFunction some Function) — so this is a clean-inheritance choice, not a
deprecation workaround: keeping no local range means pidsys:hasMember
tracks the core automatically if hasFunctionalPart's range ever changes.
(An earlier draft, checking the 2023 WD, treated FunctionalObject as
deprecated and framed this as avoiding a deprecated pin; that flag was
WD-only and does not hold in the pinned 4.2.) Do not add an explicit range.

PROJECTION CONTRACT (required, not optional): hasMember points at a
component's FUNCTIONAL individual (the type hasFunctionalPart ranges on),
never at the physical component itself — a physical piping component (a
gate valve, a pipe) is a PhysicalObject and enters a system through its
FUNCTION, not through installedAs. NOTE (corrected 2026-09-15 against the
WD): ido:installedAs is NOT the physical/functional bridge — the WD defines
it as Domain lis:InstalledObject, Range lis:PrescriptiveObject, Asymmetric:
the tag-vs-serial-numbered-artefact relation ("Pump P-101 is a tag; ACME
serial 457636 installedAs this tag"). The physical→functional link is the
function-realization pattern: the functional individual is 'lis:hasFunction
some lis:Function'; that Function (a lis:Potential) is 'lis:realizedIn' some
lis:Activity (WD: realizedIn is Domain Potential, Range Activity) in which
the physical object participates — realizedIn does NOT link a function
straight to a physical object. So rdf_mapper.py must, per member: (a) assert
the physical component, (b) mint its functional individual, (c) relate them
through the hasFunction / realizedIn / participation pattern, and (d) assert
system pidsys:hasMember functional. Pointing hasMember straight at the
physical component would wrongly infer that component to be a functional
individual. If the projection cannot yet mint functional individuals, use
pidsys:hasBoundary-style direct physical edges instead.""" .

pidsys:hasBoundary
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:CommissioningSystem ;
    rdfs:range pidsys:PipingComponent ;
    rdfs:label "has boundary" ;
    rdfs:comment "The boundary-forming components delimiting a computed system." .

#################################################################
#  Boundary role (spec §3.3) — the DEFINED class stays here;
#  the role SET (isolation/positive/relief/trap) stays SKOS
#  reference data in graph:refdata, NOT hardcoded here.
#################################################################

pidsys:BoundaryFormingComponent
    a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf pidsys:PipingComponent ;
    rdfs:label "Boundary-Forming Component" ;
    rdfs:comment """A component whose resolved class is in one of the four
boundary role sets. Defined-class semantics, but the membership LIST is
governed SKOS reference data (load_boundary_roles reads graph:refdata's
isolation/positive/relief/trap sets) — the reasoner marks the boundary,
the sets are never baked into the T-Box. CheckValve is boundary-forming in
no role set, by construction.""" .

pidsys:hasBoundaryRole
    a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain pidsys:PipingComponent ;
    rdfs:range skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "has boundary role" ;
    rdfs:comment """Links a component to its boundary role concept
(isolation / positive / relief / trap) held as SKOS refdata. Kept as an
object property to a Concept precisely so the role vocabulary stays data,
not classes.""" .

#################################################################
#  RDL resolution markers (Gold spec §4.3 / addendum) — declared
#  here because they are pidsys: predicates the projection emits
#  on domain classes.
#################################################################

pidsys:rdlUriPending
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "RDL URI pending" ;
    rdfs:comment """Asserted true on any domain class not yet carrying a
real RDL/PLM URI. Honest placeholder, cleared by a graph:refdata crosswalk
lookup — never a fabricated URI.""" .

pidsys:rdlMatchType
    a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "RDL match type" ;
    rdfs:comment """Confidence marker on a resolved class: exact | close |
label. A close/label resolution on a boundary-forming class must pass a
human review gate before feeding trusted systemization.""" .
